(582117, 58193, 14144, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by crude_art:</b></div><hr/><i> what can i say? grayscale brings out the drama in almost every situation/scene. good pick of style. good subject. it all looks greasy and dirty but it''s beautiful. it''s like a worn princess telling me how pretty and glamorous she once was. now she''s just shabby but beautifully dramatic. </i></td></tr></table></td>', 'Originally posted by crude_art: what can i say? grayscale brings out the drama in almost every situation/scene. good pick of style. good subject. it all looks greasy and dirty but it''s beautiful. it''s like a worn princess telling me how pretty and glamorous she once was. now she''s just shabby but beautifully dramatic. ', '2004-02-16 16:39:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(582176, 58406, 9411, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club.<br/> <br/>hmmm, it''s a nice shot of the stuff in your garage. I''m afraid it really doesn''t fit any definition of art that I can conjure up.<br/> <br/>Good lighting, you can see everything. Composition, umm <br/> <br/>Oh,, I have no real constructive things to say here.<br/> <br/>This really feels like a snapshot with little thought to working within the parameters of the challenge.<br/> <br/>Sorry.</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club. hmmm, it''s a nice shot of the stuff in your garage. I''m afraid it really doesn''t fit any definition of art that I can conjure up. Good lighting, you can see everything. Composition, umm  Oh,, I have no real constructive things to say here. This really feels like a snapshot with little thought to working within the parameters of the challenge. Sorry.', '2004-02-16 17:17:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(582395, 57606, 17338, '<td valign="top">The one thing I have noticed about these type of shots (Steven Spielburgish, Low angle, shooting high) is that most of them are never level. Always a tilt one way or the other. Yours tilts to the right.<br/> <br/>While everyone knows the "four corners" are N, S, E, W, the vane obliterated N and E.<br/> <br/><i>I took this picture while waiting for my daughter to get of the bus. </i> <br/> <br/>So you have time to study on how to get all of the elements lined up where everything is just right.<br/> <br/>The mood of the picture is great. The darkening clouds and the silhouette of the vane just emphasizes the coming storm.<br/> <br/>And lastly, the Challenge you entered this photo in, Garage Art, does not fit the theme. It said "Created IN, not sitting ON.<br/> <br/>Details...<br/> <br/>From a Critique Club Member<br/> <br/>Dale</td>', 'The one thing I have noticed about these type of shots (Steven Spielburgish, Low angle, shooting high) is that most of them are never level. Always a tilt one way or the other. Yours tilts to the right. While everyone knows the "four corners" are N, S, E, W, the vane obliterated N and E. I took this picture while waiting for my daughter to get of the bus.   So you have time to study on how to get all of the elements lined up where everything is just right. The mood of the picture is great. The darkening clouds and the silhouette of the vane just emphasizes the coming storm. And lastly, the Challenge you entered this photo in, Garage Art, does not fit the theme. It said "Created IN, not sitting ON. Details... From a Critique Club Member Dale', '2004-02-16 19:13:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(582639, 58415, 16591, '<td valign="top">thnx for all the comments, i learn certain things which i do not aware of.</td>', 'thnx for all the comments, i learn certain things which i do not aware of.', '2004-02-16 21:44:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(582693, 58520, 3540, '<td valign="top">Thanks everyone for all the comments.  This title was inspired by my son, who was just born Wednesday Feb. 11th.  I knew it wouldn''t really be understood by the voters, but one way or the other, I thought it was a pretty cute title and thought that no matter what my situation was, it would still fit the picture.  I''m very surprised by the amount of comments I got on this picture.  This was not the picture I wanted to take for this challenge, but I was unable to execute the photo that I really wanted to do.  So I''ll save the idea for the next applicable challenge.  Thanks again all.</td>', 'Thanks everyone for all the comments.  This title was inspired by my son, who was just born Wednesday Feb. 11th.  I knew it wouldn''t really be understood by the voters, but one way or the other, I thought it was a pretty cute title and thought that no matter what my situation was, it would still fit the picture.  I''m very surprised by the amount of comments I got on this picture.  This was not the picture I wanted to take for this challenge, but I was unable to execute the photo that I really wanted to do.  So I''ll save the idea for the next applicable challenge.  Thanks again all.', '2004-02-16 22:36:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(583114, 57673, 16362, '<td valign="top">its ok im happy<br/>Thx all u who commentet i love u all</td>', 'its ok im happyThx all u who commentet i love u all', '2004-02-17 09:30:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(583231, 57911, 14317, '<td valign="top">Great photo.  I''m 100% with you on the title.  The garage IS our last dominion in the house, especially when it comes to decorating.  It''s the only thing guys of our generation have left in the house......besides the grass.</td>', 'Great photo.  I''m 100% with you on the title.  The garage IS our last dominion in the house, especially when it comes to decorating.  It''s the only thing guys of our generation have left in the house......besides the grass.', '2004-02-17 10:58:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(583351, 57459, 6870, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I concur with all of the positive statements below!<br/> <br/>When I look at this image, my eyes are taken first to the wrench itself--which is what you want! It is placed at a diagonal, which creates some strong, dramatic lines that keep my interest. The background pops in next, and I enjoy the 3-D feel it gives to the image. The lighting here is gentle and pleasant, and does not create any harsh highlights. I also enjoy the visual clarity of the photo, which seems appropriate to me.<br/> <br/>Some below noted that the background distracts attention from the wrench, and this may be true to some extent, but I still like it. Perhaps there is a way to have the wrench in focus, but the background slightly out of focus? Just something to experiment with.<br/> <br/>This is a fine photo! To improve it, I''m not sure . . . perhaps some philosophical questions might help, if you will allow me to be nit-picky. What do you want us to feel about this wrench? This photo really shows off your photographic skill--now you might want to think about how your audience should react to it? Should we love it? hate it? admire it''s usefulness? A wrench is a wrench until you communicate something about it that surprises me or that I react to emotionally. You may be able to make us react more strongly by your lighting, point of view, context, etc. (Sorry for waxing metaphysical on you!).<br/> <br/>Very nice photo! Keep up the good work!<br/> <br/>David</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! I concur with all of the positive statements below! When I look at this image, my eyes are taken first to the wrench itself--which is what you want! It is placed at a diagonal, which creates some strong, dramatic lines that keep my interest. The background pops in next, and I enjoy the 3-D feel it gives to the image. The lighting here is gentle and pleasant, and does not create any harsh highlights. I also enjoy the visual clarity of the photo, which seems appropriate to me. Some below noted that the background distracts attention from the wrench, and this may be true to some extent, but I still like it. Perhaps there is a way to have the wrench in focus, but the background slightly out of focus? Just something to experiment with. This is a fine photo! To improve it, I''m not sure . . . perhaps some philosophical questions might help, if you will allow me to be nit-picky. What do you want us to feel about this wrench? This photo really shows off your photographic skill--now you might want to think about how your audience should react to it? Should we love it? hate it? admire it''s usefulness? A wrench is a wrench until you communicate something about it that surprises me or that I react to emotionally. You may be able to make us react more strongly by your lighting, point of view, context, etc. (Sorry for waxing metaphysical on you!). Very nice photo! Keep up the good work! David', '2004-02-17 12:25:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(583458, 57911, 8609, '<td valign="top">Taken on my birthday.  And a subject that is quite easy on eye.  ;-)</td>', 'Taken on my birthday.  And a subject that is quite easy on eye.  ;-)', '2004-02-17 14:24:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(583999, 58494, 12448, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>This is an interesting image in that it makes my turn my head and try to figure out what is all there.  The focus seems off just a bit, almost as if there was a slight camera shake, a sharper focus would help this image a great deal to me.  The cutting off the tires on the left also hurts the image to me.<br/> <br/>I do like the black and white use here.  Color would have been very distracting.  But the itmes hanging on the right are a bit distracting to the shot.  Perhaps if you had removed the other objects and turned one of the bikes over to offer some contrast?  I like the shot overall just needs a  bit of tweaking to me.  <br/> <br/>Good Luck In Future Challenges!<br/> <br/>Deannda<br/>DNeufer@stny.rr.com if you have any questions or want to discuss this further!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! This is an interesting image in that it makes my turn my head and try to figure out what is all there.  The focus seems off just a bit, almost as if there was a slight camera shake, a sharper focus would help this image a great deal to me.  The cutting off the tires on the left also hurts the image to me. I do like the black and white use here.  Color would have been very distracting.  But the itmes hanging on the right are a bit distracting to the shot.  Perhaps if you had removed the other objects and turned one of the bikes over to offer some contrast?  I like the shot overall just needs a  bit of tweaking to me.   Good Luck In Future Challenges! DeanndaDNeufer@stny.rr.com if you have any questions or want to discuss this further!', '2004-02-17 22:59:25.000000', NULL, 0);
